ARAnyM for the web (Emscripten)
===============================

This branch builds ARAnyM to WebAssembly so it can run in a web browser.
It has been tested booting EmuTOS and FreeMiNT (AFROS) with Emscripten 6.0.9,
in Firefox and Chromium.

This is a port in progress. It is not part of an ARAnyM release.


What works
----------

- Booting EmuTOS, and FreeMiNT with fVDI and XaAES
- Display, including guest resolution changes
- Keyboard and mouse, using the input functions described below
- Absolute mouse positioning, with no mouse grab needed

What does not work
------------------

- Sound. SDL2's Emscripten audio driver fails when started off the main
  thread, so the build uses the dummy driver.
- The automatic SDL2 input bridge. Browser input events do not reach
  SDL_PollEvent() under PROXY_TO_PTHREAD. The embedding page must send
  input through the functions below instead.
- Networking, host CD-ROM, USB, NatFeat exec and the X11 clipboard.
  These are disabled at configure time.
- JIT. The build uses the interpreter with normal addressing.


Build requirements
------------------

- Emscripten SDK (emsdk), activated in the current shell
- A native C compiler, for the build68k and gencpu host tools
- autoconf and automake, to generate configure
- An EmuTOS ROM image (etos1024k.img or similar)


Building
--------

1. Generate configure without running it:

       NO_CONFIGURE=1 ./autogen.sh

   Running configure in the source directory breaks the out-of-tree build
   in the next step.

2. Configure in a separate build directory:

       mkdir build-wasm && cd build-wasm
       emconfigure ../configure --host=wasm32-unknown-none \
         --disable-usbhost --disable-nfexec --disable-hostfs \
         --disable-ata-cdrom --disable-ethernet --disable-nfclipbrd \
         --enable-addressing=normal \
         CFLAGS="-O2 -pthread" CXXFLAGS="-O2 -pthread"

   Remove --disable-hostfs if you need a host file system drive.

3. Generate sources that `make aranym` does not reach on its own:

       make version_date.h
       (cd src/uae_cpu && emmake make cpudefs.cpp cpuemu.cpp \
         cpustbl.cpp cpufunctbl.cpp cputbl.h)

4. Put the ROM and a config file in build-wasm/src, then link:

       cd src
       export EMCC_CFLAGS="-pthread -fwasm-exceptions \
         -s ALLOW_MEMORY_GROWTH=1 -s INITIAL_MEMORY=134217728 \
         -s USE_PTHREADS=1 -s PTHREAD_POOL_SIZE=8 -s PROXY_TO_PTHREAD=1 \
         -s EXPORTED_RUNTIME_METHODS=ccall \
         --preload-file emutos-aranym.img@/home/web_user/.aranym/emutos-aranym.img \
         --preload-file aranym-config@/home/web_user/.aranym/config"
       emmake make aranym

   This produces aranym (the JavaScript loader), aranym.wasm and
   aranym.data. Rename aranym to aranym.js if your server needs the
   extension.

Why these options are needed
----------------------------

--host=wasm32-unknown-none
    The bundled config.sub does not recognise wasm32-unknown-emscripten.

--enable-addressing=normal
    Direct and fixed addressing need mmap(MAP_FIXED), which WebAssembly
    does not have. configure picks direct addressing by default when
    cross-compiling.

-pthread, USE_PTHREADS, PROXY_TO_PTHREAD
    SDL2 needs real threads for condition variables. ARAnyM's main loop
    blocks, so main() must run on a worker thread. Every object must be
    built with -pthread, so do a clean rebuild after changing it.

-fwasm-exceptions
    The 68k CPU emulation uses C++ exceptions. Emscripten turns them off
    by default. ASYNCIFY cannot be used instead, as it does not support
    WebAssembly exceptions.

EXPORTED_RUNTIME_METHODS=ccall
    Lets the page call the input functions.

--preload-file .../config
    If no config file exists, ARAnyM opens its setup screen instead of
    booting. The file can be almost empty.

EMCC_CFLAGS
    Pass Emscripten options this way, not in CFLAGS or LDFLAGS. This keeps
    them away from the native build68k and gencpu tools.


Running in a page
-----------------

The page must be cross-origin isolated, because threads need
SharedArrayBuffer. Serve it with these headers:

    Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy: same-origin
    Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy: require-corp

The page must contain a canvas with id="canvas". SDL resizes the canvas
using that selector, but draws to Module.canvas. If they are different
elements, resolution changes crop the display and mouse positions are
wrong.

Set up Module before loading aranym.js with a script tag:

    <canvas id="canvas" width="640" height="480"></canvas>
    <script>
      var Module = {
        canvas: document.getElementById("canvas"),
        arguments: ["-e"],   // boot EmuTOS
        preRun: [function () { ENV.SDL_AUDIODRIVER = "dummy"; }],
        locateFile: function (path) { return "/cores/aranym/" + path; },
        onRuntimeInitialized: function () {
          Module.ccall("web_set_grabbed", null, ["number"], [1]);
        }
      };
    </script>
    <script src="/cores/aranym/aranym.js"></script>

Hide the browser pointer over the canvas (cursor: none) when using
absolute mouse positioning, as the guest draws its own.


Input functions
---------------

Call these from JavaScript with Module.ccall. Each one queues the event on
ARAnyM's runtime thread and returns straight away.

web_set_grabbed(grabbed)
    Marks the mouse as grabbed (1) or released (0). ARAnyM ignores mouse
    input while released. Call web_set_grabbed(1) once at start-up. No
    pointer lock is needed.

web_inject_abs_mouse_position(x, y)
    Moves the guest mouse to an absolute position. x and y run from 0 to
    65535 across the canvas, whatever its size. Send one call per
    mousemove, and send the last position again about 150ms after the
    pointer stops. See "How absolute positioning works".

web_inject_mouse_motion(xrel, yrel)
    Moves the guest mouse by a relative amount, in host pixels. Use this
    with pointer lock instead of absolute positioning.

web_inject_mouse_button(button, down)
    Presses (1) or releases (0) a button: 1 left, 2 middle, 3 right.
    Send the pointer position first.

web_inject_key(scancode, down)
    Presses (1) or releases (0) a key. scancode is an SDL_Scancode. These
    follow USB HID usage numbers, as KeyboardEvent.code values do, so a
    lookup table from code to scancode is enough. Keys ARAnyM does not map
    are ignored.

web_set_abs_mouse_debug(enabled)
    Turns logging of absolute mouse handling to stderr on (1) or off (0).
    For debugging only.


How absolute positioning works
------------------------------

The IKBD absolute mouse commands are not implemented in ARAnyM, so
web_inject_abs_mouse_position() sends relative IKBD movement instead.

While the mouse is moving, it sends the change since the previous call.
Once no movement has been sent for 100ms and the guest has read all
queued movement, it sends the difference between the requested position
and the guest's actual cursor position instead. This corrects any drift,
for example from the guest cursor stopping at a screen edge. The guest's
position is read from the line-A variables GCURX and GCURY, which EmuTOS
reports at boot through NF_CONFIG.

Comparing against the guest position on every call does not work: the
guest position lags behind queued IKBD packets, so the cursor overshoots.

Movement and button presses are queued and sent to the IKBD one packet
at a time (at most 63 pixels per axis), each only once the guest has
read the previous one. FreeMiNT with fVDI applies the second of two
back-to-back mouse packets twice, so sending a large move as several
packets at once makes the cursor overshoot.

If the canvas size and ARAnyM's video size differ, a warning is written
to stderr.
